#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "648e9e50-03bf-56ba-a4ad-caf142c9628a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14574" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2-2x + 3}\\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2-2x + 3 & = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\\\[3pt] & = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\\\[3pt] & = (x-1)^2+2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 2x + 3}dx &= ∫(1)/((x - 1)^{2} + 2)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫(1)/(1 + ((x - 1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arctan u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 2x + 3}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}∫(1)/(1 + ((x - 1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{2}}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "77952d82-6a9b-5a6b-921e-32cbe82ea298" :question/math-academy-id "e-14575" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 4x^{2} &= - (4x^{2} - 4x) \\\\\n&= - ((2x)^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2x + 1 - 1) \\\\\n&= - ((2x - 1)^{2} - 1) \\\\\n&= 1 - (2x - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^{2}}}dx &= ∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (2x - 1)^{2}})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x - 1)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$u =2x - 1$.\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac12\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^{2}}}dx &= ∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (2x - 1)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin (2x - 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "4e0188f0-4d2b-5f3c-b1bd-12270e3adc0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14576" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\mid x-1 \\mid \\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2-2x&= x^2 -2x +(1-1) \\\\[3pt] &= (x^2 -2x +1)-1 \\\\[3pt] &= (x-1)^2-1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}x-1 & \\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\mid x-1\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-1$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n$u=x-1$.\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n| $x$ | $1+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}x-1 & \\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3}\\dfrac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}x-1 & \\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\int_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\dfrac{1}{\\mid u\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\textrm{arcsec}\\, {u}\\Big\\end{vmatrix}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\textrm{arcsec} \\left(2\\right) - \\textrm{arcsec}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068430 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "4b6820ef-32a8-5d26-84f6-a464db90129a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14577" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{36 -(3x - 2)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(1)/(\\sqrt{36 - (3x - 2)^{2}})dx &= ∫(1)/(\\sqrt{36(1 - (3x - 2)^{2}/36)})dx \\\\\n&= ∫(1)/(\\sqrt{36} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - (3x - 2)^{2}/36})dx \\\\\n&= ∫(1)/(6\\sqrt{1 - (3x - 2)^{2}/36})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (3x - 2)^{2}/36})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - ((3x - 2)/6)^{2}})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(1)/(\\sqrt{36 - (3x - 2)^{2}})dx &= \\frac{1}{6}∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - ((3x - 2)/6)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot 2du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\arcsin u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\arcsin (\\frac{3x - 2}{6}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "975fa508-bc91-5946-bd33-73148d6865ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{4}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065489} {:answer/id #uuid "e713076d-a815-50dc-9bba-50df0b48aebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065490} {:answer/id #uuid "f16bd10c-a0ba-51ec-8204-16f27b3adf1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065491} {:answer/id #uuid "610040b2-a83f-5888-9b50-41614b5e709a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (2x - 3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065492} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea0c985-2f21-52c3-b3fa-e0da19d227bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (2x - 3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e713076d-a815-50dc-9bba-50df0b48aebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065490} :answer-field/id #uuid "92f0771a-0095-5b48-b0c3-545dd56e20f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065488}] :question/id #uuid "772ac28e-e690-51b7-a882-989bee35e089" :question/math-academy-id "q-114906" :question/problem "What is $∫(1)/((2x - 3)^{2} + 4)dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1577cfd-1e25-5cb7-9d6a-87540a7fd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066155} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2dbf11-17b5-555a-8e31-2c202a10a3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066156} {:answer/id #uuid "24cf40d4-b719-5709-a125-8b18ffff6f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066157} {:answer/id #uuid "3d407f86-c506-5087-b4ac-1638a8651a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066158} {:answer/id #uuid "7482f316-63e5-5578-8283-9a99334dec41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/mat